sharing daily B2C scaling strategies. founder of @tryviewtrack\nTEXT https://t.co/ZwTtIlXk96\nPROFILE ErnestoSOFTWARE Ernesto Lopez {'followers': 58728, 'following': 794, 'likes': 13792, 'statuses': 7090, 'media_count': 1917}\nXURL https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2019920378940842033\nXURL https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE\nTEXT I own a few apps. currently at $200k MRR, sharing daily B2C scaling strategies. founder of @tryviewtrack\nTEXT https://t.co/cp0nJhRkPX\n\n x.woody-research-articles.json\nPROFILE woody_research Woody {'followers': 19382, 'following': 49, 'likes': 10058, 'statuses': 7140, 'media_count': 2258}\nXURL https://x.com/woody_research/status/2071981134896849206\nBLOCK Shopify + Claude is not about product descriptions.\nBLOCK That is the part everyone sees first, so that is the part everyone copies.\nBLOCK One store went from $16K in month 2 to $41K in month 3 and $91,340 in month 4. Net profit after product costs, fees and ads was $53,180.\nBLOCK The product did not suddenly become magical. The homepage did not win because it looked expensive. The store worked because Claude stopped being used as a writing tool and became the operating layer behind the business.\nBLOCK The mistake\nBLOCK Most people open Claude from scratch every time. One chat for product copy. One chat for ad hooks. One chat for customer emails. One chat for analytics.\nBLOCK Nothing connects, so nothing compounds.\nBLOCK That is the mistake.\nBLOCK A Shopify store is not a pile of outputs. It is the same decisions repeated every week: what to test, what to kill, what to fix, what to scale and what to stop trusting.\nBLOCK Random chats give you random answers. Saved loops give the store memory.\nBLOCK The new model runs on saved decisions\nBLOCK The store was not built around one magic prompt. It was built around six repeatable loops.\nBLOCK market_gap.prompt found the angle competitors were missing.\nBLOCK supplier_kill.prompt caught logistics problems before customers did.\nBLOCK page_truth.prompt turned the product page into objection handling instead of catalog copy.\nBLOCK creative_testing.prompt created ad angles before money was wasted on traffic.\nBLOCK retention_engine.prompt made the second order cheaper than the first.\nBLOCK sunday_operator.prompt read the store data every week and told the owner what to fix next.\nBLOCK That is the difference between using Claude as a writer and using Claude as the operator. A writer gives you copy. An operator keeps asking the same hard questions until the store stops leaking money.\nBLOCK Find pain before products\nBLOCK Most beginners start with the product.\nBLOCK That is already too late.\nBLOCK They find something trending, look at the top seller, copy the same page, use the same angle and launch a weaker version of a store that already exists.\nBLOCK Claude should not be used to copy the winner. It should be used to find the part of the market the winner is ignoring.\nBLOCK The input is simple: three competitor product pages, twenty bad reviews and the product you want to sell.\nBLOCK The output is not a description. The output is the gap.\nBLOCK The point is not to make Claude sound smart. The point is to make it kill weak ideas before Shopify takes your first dollar.\nBLOCK Most people only use AI after they already decided to build. This loop uses AI before the decision. That is why it saves money.\nBLOCK Kill bad suppliers before launch\nBLOCK Once the gap is clear, the next question is simple:\nBLOCK does this product actually deserve a store?\nBLOCK That is where most beginners get the order wrong.\nBLOCK They build the page, launch the ads, wait for sales, then discover the supplier is slow, the product page does not answer the real objection, the ad angle is weak, support is messy, and nobody knows what to fix next week.\nBLOCK Claude should catch those problems before they become expensive.\nBLOCK The first decision finds the market gap.\nBLOCK The next five decisions decide whether the store deserves more money.\nBLOCK Most Shopify stores do not die because the product is impossible to sell. They die because the supplier destroys trust after the sale.\nBLOCK Late delivery. Fake tracking. Bad packaging. Product quality that looked fine in the listing and terrible in the customer’s hand. By the time the owner sees the pattern, refunds are already open and Stripe is already nervous.\nBLOCK The supplier loop is simple: make Claude audit the supplier before the first order.\nBLOCK That prompt does not make the business more exciting. It makes the business harder to destroy.\nBLOCK Turn product pages into sales calls\nBLOCK After the supplier survives, the next leak is the product page.\nBLOCK Most pages sound like AliExpress rewritten in better English. “Premium quality.” “Perfect for everyone.” “Designed for comfort.” None of that answers the buyer’s real question.\nBLOCK The buyer wants to know: will this work for me, what could go wrong, why should I trust this store, and what am I not being told?\nBLOCK That is why page_truth.prompt does not write a normal product description. It turns the page into objection handling.\nBLOCK The best section is usually not what the product does. It is what the product does not do.\nBLOCK That line makes the page feel honest, and honest pages convert better than pages trying too hard.\nBLOCK Test creatives before buying attention\nBLOCK Once the page has an angle, the ad should not be one perfect script. That is another beginner mistake.\nBLOCK A good store does not test “an ad.” It tests angles.\nBLOCK One angle shows the pain. One shows the failed alternative. One shows the product in use. One answers the objection. One shows the result.\nBLOCK Claude’s job is to create those angles before ad spend starts.\nBLOCK This changes how ads are tested. The question stops being “is this product good?” The question becomes “which angle makes the buyer recognize their own problem fastest?”\nBLOCK That is a much better test.\nBLOCK Make support part of retention\nBLOCK The first sale is expensive because traffic costs money. The second sale is where the store starts breathing.\nBLOCK Most beginners ignore this. They spend all their energy getting the first order, then send the same default Shopify emails everyone else sends. That leaves money on the table.\nBLOCK retention_engine.prompt turns the customer journey into a simple sequence: confirm the order, set expectations, teach one useful thing, check in after delivery, ask for a review and introduce the next product only when it makes sense.\nBLOCK This is not the exciting part of Shopify. That is why it works.\nBLOCK Most competitors are still chasing the next click while the better store is quietly increasing the value of the buyers it already paid for.\nBLOCK Audit the store every Sunday\nBLOCK The final loop is the one that makes the system compound.\nBLOCK Every Sunday, the owner exports Shopify analytics and gives Claude the numbers. Not for motivation. For diagnosis.\nBLOCK Sessions. Conversion rate. Average order value. Cart abandonment. Returning visitors. Top products. Return rate. Ad spend. Revenue from paid traffic. ROAS.\nBLOCK Then Claude answers the same questions every week: where is the leak, what should scale, what should stop, is paid traffic finding buyers or just curious people, and what breaks first if nothing changes?\nBLOCK This is the loop most people skip because it feels boring. But this is where the compounding happens.\nBLOCK The store does not improve because the owner has a new idea every morning. It improves because the same five or six decisions get reviewed every week with better context.\nBLOCK That is the real Shopify + Claude system. Not a chatbot. Not a product description generator. A store operator made out of saved loops.\nBLOCK The math only works when the system repeats\nBLOCK By month 2, the store was doing $16K in revenue. By month 3, it reached $41K. By month 4, the dashboard showed $91,340, with $53,180 left after product costs, Shopify fees and ad spend.\nBLOCK That kind of jump makes people ask the wrong question.\nBLOCK They ask, “What product was it?”\nBLOCK The better question is, “What system kept improving the store every week?”\nBLOCK Because the product did not go from average to magical between month 2 and month 4. The operating layer got sharper. The supplier risk got lower. The page answered better objections. The ads tested clearer angles. The emails recovered more value from buyers who already paid. The Sunday audit stopped the owner from guessing what to fix next.\nBLOCK None of those changes looks dramatic alone. Together, they change the economics of the store.\nBLOCK A 1% better product page, a better opening shot, fewer refund issues, cleaner support replies and one useful weekly fix do not look like a $91K/month system when you see them separately.\nBLOCK But inside a Shopify store, small leaks are where the profit disappears.\nBLOCK The loops did not create the revenue by magic. They stopped the store from bleeding it out.\nBLOCK What Claude still cannot fix\nBLOCK The wrong lesson is that Claude makes Shopify easy.\nBLOCK It does not.\nBLOCK You still need a product people want, a supplier that can deliver, traffic that is not junk and margins that survive refunds, fees and ad spend.\nBLOCK Claude can help you see those things faster. It cannot make them true.\nBLOCK That is the difference between using AI as a shortcut and using it as an operating system.\nBLOCK A shortcut says: “write my page so this sells.”\nBLOCK An operating system says: “tell me if this should exist, where it will break, what buyers will doubt, what angle to test and what the numbers say after one week.”\nBLOCK The first one gives you a cleaner guess. The second one gives you a store that learns.\nBLOCK The next Shopify team will look like a folder of prompts\nBLOCK The old Shopify playbook was find a product, build a page, run ads and hope the numbers worked.\nBLOCK The new playbook is turning the store into repeatable decisions: what to test, what to kill, what to fix, what to scale and what to stop trusting.\nBLOCK Claude is useful when those decisions stop living in the owner’s head and start living in the system.\nBLOCK Shopify gives you the shelf. Claude gives you the operator. The store wins when the loop keeps improving.\nTEXT Shopify + Claude is not about product descriptions.\nTEXT That is the part everyone sees first, so that is the part everyone copies.\nTEXT One store went from $16K in month 2 to $41K in month 3 and $91,340 in month 4. Net profit after product costs, fees and ads was $53,180.\nTEXT The product did not suddenly become magical. The homepage did not win because it looked expensive. The store worked because Claude stopped being used as a writing tool and became the operating layer behind the business.\nTEXT The mistake\nTEXT Most people open Claude from scratch every time. One chat for product copy. One chat for ad hooks. One chat for customer emails. One chat for analytics.\nTEXT Nothing connects, so nothing compounds.\nTEXT That is the mistake.\nTEXT A Shopify store is not a pile of outputs. It is the same decisions repeated every week: what to test, what to kill, what to fix, what to scale and what to stop trusting.\nTEXT Random chats give you random answers. Saved loops give the store memory.\nTEXT The new model runs on saved decisions\nTEXT The store was not built around one magic prompt. It was built around six repeatable loops.\nTEXT market_gap.prompt found the angle competitors were missing.\nTEXT supplier_kill.prompt caught logistics problems before customers did.\nTEXT page_truth.prompt turned the product page into objection handling instead of catalog copy.\nTEXT creative_testing.prompt created ad angles before money was wasted on traffic.\nTEXT retention_engine.prompt made the second order cheaper than the first.\nTEXT sunday_operator.prompt read the store data every w