ner sees the pattern, refunds are already open and Stripe is already nervous.\nTEXT The supplier loop is simple: make Claude audit the supplier before the first order.\nTEXT That prompt does not make the business more exciting. It makes the business harder to destroy.\nTEXT Turn product pages into sales calls\nTEXT After the supplier survives, the next leak is the product page.\nTEXT Most pages sound like AliExpress rewritten in better English. “Premium quality.” “Perfect for everyone.” “Designed for comfort.” None of that answers the buyer’s real question.\nTEXT The buyer wants to know: will this work for me, what could go wrong, why should I trust this store, and what am I not being told?\nTEXT That is why page_truth.prompt does not write a normal product description. It turns the page into objection handling.\nTEXT The best section is usually not what the product does. It is what the product does not do.\nTEXT That line makes the page feel honest, and honest pages convert better than pages trying too hard.\nTEXT Test creatives before buying attention\nTEXT Once the page has an angle, the ad should not be one perfect script. That is another beginner mistake.\nTEXT A good store does not test “an ad.” It tests angles.\nTEXT One angle shows the pain. One shows the failed alternative. One shows the product in use. One answers the objection. One shows the result.\nTEXT Claude’s job is to create those angles before ad spend starts.\nTEXT This changes how ads are tested. The question stops being “is this product good?” The question becomes “which angle makes the buyer recognize their own problem fastest?”\nTEXT That is a much better test.\nTEXT Make support part of retention\nTEXT The first sale is expensive because traffic costs money. The second sale is where the store starts breathing.\nTEXT Most beginners ignore this. They spend all their energy getting the first order, then send the same default Shopify emails everyone else sends. That leaves money on the table.\nTEXT retention_engine.prompt turns the customer journey into a simple sequence: confirm the order, set expectations, teach one useful thing, check in after delivery, ask for a review and introduce the next product only when it makes sense.\nTEXT This is not the exciting part of Shopify. That is why it works.\nTEXT Most competitors are still chasing the next click while the better store is quietly increasing the value of the buyers it already paid for.\nTEXT Audit the store every Sunday\nTEXT The final loop is the one that makes the system compound.\nTEXT Every Sunday, the owner exports Shopify analytics and gives Claude the numbers. Not for motivation. For diagnosis.\nTEXT Sessions. Conversion rate. Average order value. Cart abandonment. Returning visitors. Top products. Return rate. Ad spend. Revenue from paid traffic. ROAS.\nTEXT Then Claude answers the same questions every week: where is the leak, what should scale, what should stop, is paid traffic finding buyers or just curious people, and what breaks first if nothing changes?\nTEXT This is the loop most people skip because it feels boring. But this is where the compounding happens.\nTEXT The store does not improve because the owner has a new idea every morning. It improves because the same five or six decisions get reviewed every week with better context.\nTEXT That is the real Shopify + Claude system. Not a chatbot. Not a product description generator. A store operator made out of saved loops.\nTEXT The math only works when the system repeats\nTEXT By month 2, the store was doing $16K in revenue. By month 3, it reached $41K. By month 4, the dashboard showed $91,340, with $53,180 left after product costs, Shopify fees and ad spend.\nTEXT That kind of jump makes people ask the wrong question.\nTEXT They ask, “What product was it?”\nTEXT The better question is, “What system kept improving the store every week?”\nTEXT Because the product did not go from average to magical between month 2 and month 4. The operating layer got sharper. The supplier risk got lower. The page answered better objections. The ads tested clearer angles. The emails recovered more value from buyers who already paid. The Sunday audit stopped the owner from guessing what to fix next.\nTEXT None of those changes looks dramatic alone. Together, they change the economics of the store.\nTEXT A 1% better product page, a better opening shot, fewer refund issues, cleaner support replies and one useful weekly fix do not look like a $91K/month system when you see them separately.\nTEXT But inside a Shopify store, small leaks are where the profit disappears.\nTEXT The loops did not create the revenue by magic. They stopped the store from bleeding it out.\nTEXT What Claude still cannot fix\nTEXT The wrong lesson is that Claude makes Shopify easy.\nTEXT It does not.\nTEXT You still need a product people want, a supplier that can deliver, traffic that is not junk and margins that survive refunds, fees and ad spend.\nTEXT Claude can help you see those things faster. It cannot make them true.\nTEXT That is the difference between using AI as a shortcut and using it as an operating system.\nTEXT A shortcut says: “write my page so this sells.”\nTEXT An operating system says: “tell me if this should exist, where it will break, what buyers will doubt, what angle to test and what the numbers say after one week.”\nTEXT The first one gives you a cleaner guess. The second one gives you a store that learns.\nTEXT The next Shopify team will look like a folder of prompts\nTEXT The old Shopify playbook was find a product, build a page, run ads and hope the numbers worked.\nTEXT The new playbook is turning the store into repeatable decisions: what to test, what to kill, what to fix, what to scale and what to stop trusting.\nTEXT Claude is useful when those decisions stop living in the owner’s head and start living in the system.\nTEXT Shopify gives you the shelf. Claude gives you the operator. The store wins when the loop keeps improving.\nXURL https://x.com/woody_research\nTEXT 𝐫𝐞𝐬𝐞𝐚𝐫𝐜𝐡𝐞𝐬 | 𝐠𝐮𝐢𝐝𝐞𝐬 | 𝐢𝐧𝐯𝐞𝐬𝐭𝐦𝐞𝐧𝐭𝐬\nTEXT https://t.co/g3sMI2h38x\nPROFILE woody_research Woody {'followers': 19382, 'following': 49, 'likes': 10058, 'statuses': 7140, 'media_count': 2258}\nXURL https://x.com/woody_research/status/2061700552422195234\nBLOCK How I Built a Faceless YouTube Channel to $41k a Month With Claude\nBLOCK Eight months ago I had a laptop, a $20 Claude subscription, and no idea what I was doing. /  / No camera, no editor, no team. Last month the channel did $9,400 and I've never shown my face once. /  / The whole thing is six steps. Claude does the heavy lifting on four of them. Here's the exact build, with the prompts I actually paste.\nBLOCK First, the money\nBLOCK Everyone asks what YouTube pays per view. Wrong question. The niche decides everything:\nBLOCK Finance and investing: $15-50 RPM / Technology and AI: $12-30 / Health and longevity: $10-25 / General and entertainment: $3-8\nBLOCK  / My channel is finance. Around 480,000 views last month at an $18 RPM is about $8,600 from AdSense alone, before one affiliate link. A cooking channel at $3 RPM needs five million views for the same check. /  / Views are the engine. The niche is the fuel.\nBLOCK Step 1: Pick the niche by RPM, not by what you like\nBLOCK I picked a cooking idea first because I liked it. That mistake cost me three weeks. Now I make Claude run the math before I commit: / \nBLOCK  / It handed me \"debt payoff for people on a normal salary\" inside finance, plus a year of titles. That's a $300 consultant call for free.\nBLOCK Step 2: Write the script. This is the actual business\nBLOCK My first 5 videos flopped. Clean voice, fine footage, 200 views each. The problem was the writing. /  / YouTube only really measures one thing, do people keep watching. A script that holds 70% gets pushed to new viewers, one that holds 30% gets buried. That gap is the script, not the production. /  / This is the prompt I paste for every video:\nBLOCK  / Once I fixed the script, the same channel that did 200 views started doing 40,000. Nothing else has changed.\nBLOCK Step 3: Voice it once, keep that voice forever\nBLOCK ElevenLabs reads the script. I picked one voice and never change it, so the channel keeps an identity viewers recognize. Set the speed to 0.9x for finance, it reads with more authority. Drop [pause] markers into the script where it should breathe, that's what makes it stop sounding like a robot /  / $22 a month. This step is not where you win or lose, so don't overthink it. / \nBLOCK Step 4: Assemble it in CapCut\nBLOCK No camera means no editing, just matching footage to the voiceover. The rule: the visual changes every 3 to 5 seconds. Pexels and Pixabay cover most of the B-roll for free. /  / Turn on CapCut's auto-captions. They lifted my watch time about 15% because most people scroll with the sound off.\nBLOCK Step 5: Let Claude write the metadata\nBLOCK I had one video that deserved 80,000 views sitting at 800. Great script, clean voice, decent thumbnail. The title was terrible. /  / Now Claude writes the whole metadata layer in one shot:\nBLOCK That one prompt is worth $50 a video if you hire it out. I run it in 20 seconds.\nBLOCK Step 6: Post 20 to 30 videos before you judge anything\nBLOCK I almost quit at video 8. Nothing was moving. The channel didn't start climbing until video 24. /  / The algorithm needs 20 to 30 videos before it learns who to show you to. Most people post 8, see flat numbers, and walk away one month before the curve turns. Same day, same time, every week, no exceptions for the first six months.\nBLOCK What it costs\nBLOCK Claude: $20/mo / ElevenLabs: $22/mo / CapCut, Canva, Pexels: free / Total: ~$57/mo\nBLOCK Against what this replaces, a scriptwriter at $150-400 a video, a voice actor, an editor, an SEO person. I'm running all of it on the price of two coffees a month.\nBLOCK The part nobody tells you\nBLOCK For fifteen years the barrier to YouTube was the camera. You needed a face, a voice, presence, gear, years of reps on camera. /  / That barrier is gone, and losing it showed me something that was always true. The camera was a distraction from the actual job, which is writing things people don't want to stop watching. /  / The faceless channel didn't remove the hard part. It removed everything that was hiding it. I stopped competing on being watchable and started competing on being un-skippable. /  / That one's a writing problem, and Claude already solved it.\nTEXT How I Built a Faceless YouTube Channel to $41k a Month With Claude\nTEXT Eight months ago I had a laptop, a $20 Claude subscription, and no idea what I was doing. /  / No camera, no editor, no team. Last month the channel did $9,400 and I've never shown my face once. /  / The whole thing is six steps. Claude does the heavy lifting on four of them. Here's the exact build, with the prompts I actually paste.\nTEXT First, the money\nTEXT Everyone asks what YouTube pays per view. Wrong question. The niche decides everything:\nTEXT Finance and investing: $15-50 RPM / Technology and AI: $12-30 / Health and longevity: $10-25 / General and entertainment: $3-8\nTEXT  / My channel is finance. Around 480,000 views last month at an $18 RPM is about $8,600 from AdSense alone, before one affiliate link. A cooking channel at $3 RPM needs five million views for the same check. /  / Views are the engine. The niche is the fuel.\nTEXT Step 1: Pick the niche by RPM, not by what you like\nTEXT I picked a cooking idea first because I liked it. That mistake cost me three weeks. Now I make Claude run the math before I commit: / \nTEXT  / It handed me \"debt payoff for people on a normal salary\" inside finance, plus a year of titles. That's a $300 consultant call for free.\nTEXT Step 2: Write the script. This is the actual business\nTEXT My first 5 videos flopped. Clean voice, fine footage, 200 views each. The problem was the writing. /  / YouTube only really measures one thing, do people keep watching. A script that holds 70% gets pushed to new viewers, one that holds 30% gets buried. That gap is the script, not the production. /  / This is the prompt I paste for every video:\nTEXT  / Once I fixed the script, the same channel that did 200 views started doing 40,000. Nothing else has changed.\nTEXT Step 3: Voice it once, keep that voice forever\nTEXT ElevenLabs reads the script. I picked one voice and never change it, so the channel keeps an identity viewers recognize. Set the speed to 0.9x for finance, it reads with more authority. Drop [pause] markers into the script where it should breathe, that's what makes it stop sounding like a robot /  / $22 a month. This step is not where you win or lose, so don't overthink it. / \nTEXT Step 4: Assemble it in CapCut\nTEXT No camera means no editing, just matching footage to the voiceover. The rule: the visual changes every 3 to 5 seconds. Pexels and Pixabay cover most of the B-roll for free. /  / Turn on CapCut's auto-captions. They lifted my watch time about 15% because most people scroll with the sound off.\nTEXT Step 5: Let Claude write the metadata\nTEXT I had one video that deserved 80,000 views sitting at 800. Great script, clean voice, decent thumbnail. The title was terrible. /  / Now Claude writes the whole metadata layer in one shot:\nTEXT That one prompt is worth $50 a video if you hire it out. I run it in 20 seconds.\nTEXT Step 6: Post 20 to 30 videos before you judge anything\nTEXT I almost quit at video 8. Nothing was moving. The channel didn't start climbing until video 24. /  / The algorithm needs 20 to 30 videos before it learns who to show you to. Most people post 8, see flat numbers, and walk away one month before the curve turns. Same day, same time, every week, no exceptions for the first six months.\nTEXT What it costs\nTEXT Claude: $20/mo / ElevenLabs: $22/mo / CapCut, Canva, Pexels: free / Total: ~$57/mo\nTEXT Against what this replaces, a scriptwriter at $150-400 a video, a voice actor, an editor, an SEO person. I'm running all of it on the price of two coffees a month.\nTEXT The part nobody tells you\nTEXT For fifteen years the barrier to YouTube was the camera. You needed a face, a voice, presence, gear, years of reps on camera. /  / That barrier is gone, and losing it showed me something that was always true. The camera was a distraction from the actual job, which is writing things people don't want to stop watching. /  / The faceless channel didn't remove the hard part. It removed everything that was hiding it. I stopped competing on being watchable and started competing on being un-skippable. /  / That one's a writing problem, and Claude already solved it.\nXURL https://x.com/woody_research\nTEXT 𝐫𝐞𝐬𝐞𝐚𝐫𝐜𝐡𝐞𝐬 | 𝐠𝐮𝐢𝐝𝐞𝐬 | 𝐢𝐧𝐯𝐞𝐬𝐭𝐦𝐞𝐧𝐭𝐬\nTEXT https://t.co/p27zuGuqnV\nPROFILE woody_research Woody {'followers': 19382, 'following': 49, 'likes': 10058, 'statuses': 7140, 'media_count': 2258}\nXURL https://x.com/woody_research/status/2021358286705192975\nBLOCK Stumbled on a Polymarket wallet pulling / $700k+ in a month.\nBLOCK No noise. / No bot promo. / No “AI made me rich” crap.\nBLOCK But the way this wallet trades? / Yeah… it smells like Clawbot logic all over it.\nBLOCK Here’s what’s wild:\nBLOCK • $23.7M traded / • 259 preds / • Biggest hit: $274k / • PnL just goes up — no cliffs, no nukes\nBLOCK Not degen gambling. / This is someone treating Polymarket like infra. /  / Most people trade opinions. / This guy trades conditions.\nBLOCK Big single entries. / No DCA. / No tilt. / Waits forever… then hits size.\nBLOCK Sports markets (NHL heavy). / Odds lag → money moves.\nBLOCK Honestly, if Clawbot had a “manual mode”, it’d look like this:\nBLOCK That’s it. / No fancy indicators. / No 5k trades/day.\nBLOCK Just letting Polymarket be slow.\nBLOCK That’s the edge nobody talks about.\nBLOCK Polymarket doesn’t punish you for being right. / It punishes you for being late.\nBLOCK This wallet wasn’t early by seconds. / It was early by thinking. /  / Ever