{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,glob,os,re\nfor p in glob.glob('/Users/anicca/anicca/skills/earn/marketing-engine/evidence/intel/pulls/68f5220fe826411ab0d41408bda1f09e/x.*.json'):\n d=json.load(open(p)); author=None; urls=[]; blocks=[]\n def rec(x):\n  nonlocal_dummy=None\n  global author\n  if isinstance(x,dict):\n   if isinstance(x.get('author'),dict) and x['author'].get('screen_name') and 'followers' in x['author']:\n    globals()['author']=x['author']\n   if isinstance(x.get('url'),str) and '/status/' in x['url'] and x['url'] not in urls: urls.append(x['url'])\n   if 'content' in x and isinstance(x['content'],dict):\n    for b in x['content'].get('blocks',[]):\n     t=b.get('text','').strip()\n     if t and len(blocks)<15: blocks.append(t)\n   for v in x.values(): rec(v)\n  elif isinstance(x,list):\n   for v in x: rec(v)\n rec(d)\n print(os.path.basename(p), author and {k:author.get(k) for k in ['screen_name','name','followers','following','likes','statuses','media_count','url']}, urls)\n print(' | '.join(blocks[:12]))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"x.ernesto-software-articles.json {'screen_name': 'ErnestoSOFTWARE', 'name': 'Ernesto Lopez', 'followers': 58728, 'following': 794, 'likes': 13792, 'statuses': 7090, 'media_count': 1917, 'url': 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE'} ['https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2082527284301300023', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2067994710841360596', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2062582178236727320', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2056740238890766383', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2055380685452640538', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2047111389890629654', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2038622376640864287', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2037187494530208029', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2033917717762191659', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2028947872847413388', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2027105511569809899', 'https://x.com/ErnestoSOFTWARE/status/2019920378940842033']\nOur app is currently doing $50,000/week. \nThis month we will wrap up at $150K MRR. | and thats just 1 of my businesses, I have have many others \n\nbringing in over $200k/mo in personal income.and all of these businesses are built on the back of claude. | Claude is literally my COO. | and yet 99% of people still use Claude like an assistant,\nto help them with basic tasks.\n\nat this point thats caveman behavior.\n\nThe reason i'm able to run so many \n6 figure/mo business at once | boils down to the precision in which I use Claude. \nand thats exactly what i will break down in this article | STEP 1: MCP MAXING | The first step to get above average results with Claude\nIs to give claude access to useful MCPS:\n\nMCP's are like super powers for Claude\nFor example, recently i tried re creating a very famous game\n\nThat has over 4 Billion downloads\nI sent the same prompt to 2 agents\n\n1 claude agent had access to 2 MCPS   \n( higgsfield MCP for images + meshy AI for 3d models )\n\nThe other agent had no access to mcps. | The results speak for themselves.\n\nthe agent with access to MCP delivered exceptional results \ncompared to the other agent. | SO WHICH MCPS SHOULD YOU GET? | Lets say you want to build a  SAAS that turns images into 3D models.\nI'll send Claude a prompt like this: | My Claude code agent is connected to all of these MCPS by default:\n\n→  Dribbble API to research visual direction\n→ Higgsfield MCP for every image gen and video gen model\n→ Blender MCP to make 3D assets for games\n→ Vercel MCP to host the website\n→ Resend API to send emails (login codes, receipts, everything)\n→ Supabase MCP to spin up the database\n→ GoDaddy MCP to manage DNS records | so now instead of wasting days setting \neverything up myself \n\nClaude handles it all.  \n\nbut even with all these super powers\n\nyou would still need to be sending 1 prompt at a time. \nbecause AI has a cap on how many tokens it can spend per prompt.\n\nthats when the loops comes in.\n\nCLAUDE LOOPS + MCPS = SUPER EFFICIENCY\nx.woody-research-articles.json {'screen_name': 'woody_research', 'name': 'Woody', 'followers': 19382, 'following': 49, 'likes': 10058, 'statuses': 7140, 'media_count': 2258, 'url': 'https://x.com/woody_research'} ['https://x.com/woody_research/status/2071981134896849206', 'https://x.com/woody_research/status/2061700552422195234', 'https://x.com/woody_research/status/2021358286705192975']\nShopify + Claude is not about product descriptions. | That is the part everyone sees first, so that is the part everyone copies. | One store went from $16K in month 2 to $41K in month 3 and $91,340 in month 4. Net profit after product costs, fees and ads was $53,180. | The product did not suddenly become magical. The homepage did not win because it looked expensive. The store worked because Claude stopped being used as a writing tool and became the operating layer behind the business. | The mistake | Most people open Claude from scratch every time. One chat for product copy. One chat for ad hooks. One chat for customer emails. One chat for analytics. | Nothing connects, so nothing compounds. | That is the mistake. | A Shopify store is not a pile of outputs. It is the same decisions repeated every week: what to test, what to kill, what to fix, what to scale and what to stop trusting. | Random chats give you random answers. Saved loops give the store memory. | The new model runs on saved decisions | The store was not built around one magic prompt. It was built around six repeatable loops.\nx.3imzdo3-articles.json {'screen_name': '3Imzdo3', 'name': 'とら🐯AIマネタイズ', 'followers': 1119, 'following': 50, 'likes': 2438, 'statuses': 2914, 'media_count': 138, 'url': 'https://x.com/3Imzdo3'} ['https://x.com/3Imzdo3/status/2083479993414529299', 'https://x.com/3Imzdo3/status/2083118547816100254', 'https://x.com/3Imzdo3/status/2082760469300011416', 'https://x.com/3Imzdo3/status/2082390961368535423', 'https://x.com/3Imzdo3/status/2081668498338807900']\n「何をやっても稼ぐ人」\n\nっているじゃないですか？ | 物販をやっても稼ぐ。 | SNSを始めても伸ばす。 | 投資へ移っても資産を増やす。 | 扱う商品や市場が変わっても、\n気づけば富を築いてる。 | 反対に、 | 同じ教材を買って、 | 同じ情報を学んで | 同じノウハウを学んでいるのに、 | 半年後もまだ、 | 「結局、何をやれば稼げますか？」 | と聞き続けている人がいるのも\nこのビジネス界隈。\nx.george-lampro-articles.json {'screen_name': 'GeorgeLampro20', 'name': 'George Lampropoulos', 'followers': 10305, 'following': 473, 'likes': 1263, 'statuses': 2498, 'media_count': 245, 'url': 'https://x.com/GeorgeLampro20'} ['https://x.com/GeorgeLampro20/status/2081979523873038368']\nHow to Scale Your App to $10K/Month (Easy Mode) | You clicked on this because you want to build a mobile app that actually makes money. Not a portfolio piece. Not a \"someday\" idea sitting in your notes. Real recurring revenue you can watch hit your dashboard every single morning. | Here is the good news, and I mean it: you do not need a dev team, you do not need funding, and you do not need to know how to code. I know that because I am living proof of it. I am 19. A couple of years ago I was working a register at TJ Maxx. Today I have made over $300,000 building consumer apps with AI. In the last 28 days alone I did $52,000 in revenue, and over the last 90 days I scaled two apps to $20K and $25K a month. It all started with an AI wrestling app called Wrestle AI, which did over $160,000 in its first six months and crossed 100,000 downloads. I do not know how to write a single line of code. I built all of it with AI. | People have been making mobile apps since 2008, and for most of that time it kept getting harder to build something people would actually download and pay for. Then two things changed everything. AI made it so anyone can create software, and AI made it so you can build experiences that are genuinely better than what came before. Cal AI lets you photograph a plate of spaghetti and instantly know the calories. Wrestle AI lets you upload your match and instantly know what you did wrong. Those experiences simply were not possible before, and that is the entire opportunity. | This is everything I know, laid out as a playbook. Nothing held back. If you read it top to bottom and actually do the work, you can do what I did. Let me show you how. | Table of Contents | The Only Math That Matters | Start With an Idea That Can Actually Win | How to Reverse Engineer a Viral App Idea | Why a Good Idea Beats Good Distribution | The Gotcha Moment: Your Single Most Important Asset | The Six-Step Process I Use to Build Any App\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
